<circuit version="1.1.0-SR2" rev="261109" stepSize="1000000" stepsPS="20000" NLsteps="100000" reaStep="1000000" animate="0" >
<description>
Tow-Thomas state-variable (biquad) filter: three op amps in one loop give band-pass and low-pass outputs of the same second-order filter.

How it works:
- U1 is a lossy integrator (C1 parallel RQ): its output is the band-pass (BP).
- U2 integrates BP: its output is the low-pass (LP).
- U3 inverts LP; R5 feeds -LP back to U1, which closes the loop.
- f0 = 1/(2*pi*R*C) = 1/(2*pi*10k*15.915nF) = 1.000 kHz. Q = RQ/R = 1. At f0, BP = -(RQ/R1)*Vin, and LP has the same amplitude, 90 degrees shifted.

Input: 1 kHz, 1 V peak sine (Vin). Scope: IN (yellow), BP, LP. Speed 2 %.

What to try:
- Change Vin's frequency: at 100 Hz LP follows the input (gain 1) and BP drops to 0.1; at 10 kHz both fall (BP 0.1, LP 0.01).
- Raise RQ to 50k (Q = 5): BP gets 5 times narrower and 5 times higher at f0.

Results [SIMULATED]: at 1 kHz, BP = 0.994 V peak and LP = 0.994 V peak (theory 1.000 V); no convergence faults.

Why it is here: a test circuit for the op-amp model (BH-032). Three high-gain stages in one feedback loop never converged with the old per-op-amp iteration: 3948 "Not converging" faults in 25 ms and wrong amplitudes (BP 0.18 V).
</description>


